import { +=, print, to_str } from builtins;

// `+=` has no bare form, exactly as `+` and `++` have none: the alias is what
// picks the semantics, and the grammar refuses the bare spelling with a message
// saying so.
fn main() !prints {
    mut n = 1;
    set n += 2;
    print(n.to_str());
}
--- errors ---
error: an operator has no bare import: import the builtin it stands for and alias it to the operator, e.g. `wrapping_add as +`
